'''''El Camí de l'Exili''''' ({{lang|ca|El Camí de l'Exili}}; literally "The Path of Exile") is a 1923 Volisanian-language silent film directed by [[Margarida Soler i Besora]] and produced by [[Estudis Costa Nord]]. It was the second feature film produced in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the first to address the [[Baliscan Revolution]] and the [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|exile experience]] directly. The film depicts the experience of a Baliscan family fleeing the revolution and was based on the experiences of the [[Baliscano Balboan people|Baliscano Balboan]] community in Sant Cristòfor. A strongly anti-revolutionary work, the film portrayed the pre-revolution Baliscan society as a golden age destroyed by the upheaval, and it became one of the most popular films in the Volisanian-speaking world.

== Production ==

=== Background ===

[[Margarida Soler i Besora]] was a theatre director from [[Cerdanya]], [[Balisca]], where she had been part of the pre-revolution Baliscan cultural establishment. Born in 1885, she was 33 years old when she fled Balisca during the [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|post-revolution exodus]] in 1919 and settled in [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]], Sant Cristòfor. The following year, at the age of 34, she founded [[Estudis Costa Nord]], the first film studio in the territory, financing its establishment with savings from her theatre career and contributions from fellow Baliscan exiles. Soler had been a prominent theatre director in Cerdanya, and her transition to filmmaking was driven by a desire to document the exile experience and to preserve the memory of the society that the revolution had destroyed. She is widely regarded as the "godmother of cinema" in Balboa for her pioneering role in establishing the territory's film industry.

The screenplay was co-written by Soler and [[Pere Riera i Font]], a Baliscan journalist and poet who had also fled the revolution. Riera had documented the exodus in a series of articles published in the Volisanian-language press in Sant Cristòfor, and his accounts provided the factual basis for the film's narrative. The screenplay drew on the experiences of the Baliscano Balboan community in Costa Nord, many of whom had lost family members, property, and social standing in the revolution.

=== Filming ===

The film was shot in 1922 on a modest budget provided by [[Estudis Costa Nord]]. The production used locations in [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] and [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], with the Costa Nord seafront standing in for the Baliscan coast in the opening sequences. The production could not film in Balisca itself, as the post-revolution government would not have permitted a film crew associated with the exile community to operate on Baliscan territory. Instead, the Baliscan settings were recreated using existing architecture in Sant Cristòfor that resembled the colonial style of Cerdanya, supplemented by painted backdrops for interior scenes.

The cast was drawn primarily from the Baliscano Balboan community. [[Lluís Vergés i Arimany]], who played the family patriarch, had been a stage actor in Cerdanya before the revolution. [[Caterina Puig i Mas]], who played the matriarch, was a Baliscan exile who had worked as a schoolteacher in Balisca and had no prior acting experience; she was cast by Soler for her authentic understanding of the exile experience. [[Bernat Soler i Besora]], the director's brother, played the eldest son.

== Plot ==

The film follows the [[w:Vives family|Vives family]], a prosperous middle-class family from Cerdanya, over a period of approximately two years spanning the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Revolution]] in 1918 and their subsequent flight to Sant Cristòfor. The narrative is divided into three acts.

The first act, set in Cerdanya before the revolution, depicts the Vives family's comfortable life — the father's successful business, the children's education, the family's participation in civic and religious life, and the social bonds of their community. This section of the film is deliberately idyllic, portraying pre-revolution Baliscan society as a harmonious and prosperous world. The family attends church, celebrates festivals, and gathers for meals in their large home. The father, a respected merchant, is shown dealing fairly with customers and contributing to community life.

The second act depicts the revolution's arrival in Cerdanya. The film shows the disruption of daily life — the closure of churches, the seizure of property, the dissolution of civic institutions, and the growing climate of fear and intimidation. The Vives family's business is confiscated, their church is shut down, and their neighbours begin to disappear. The father is warned by a friend that his name has appeared on a list of persons targeted by the revolutionary committees. The family makes the decision to flee.

The third act follows the family's journey into exile — the departure from their home, the difficult passage to the coast, the sea crossing, and the arrival in Sant Cristòfor. The final scenes depict the family's new life in Costa Nord, where they live in reduced circumstances, the father working as a clerk and the mother teaching piano. The film ends with the family attending a Volisanian-language church service in their new city, the father looking back toward the sea in the direction of Balisca.

== Themes ==

The film's anti-revolutionary stance was explicit and uncompromising. The contrast between the ordered prosperity of pre-revolution Balisca and the violence and dispossession of the revolution was the film's central thematic device. The first act's idyllic portrayal of Baliscan life was intended not as nostalgia but as political argument — the film presented the pre-revolution social order as a positive good that the revolution had destroyed, and it offered no suggestion that the revolution addressed any legitimate grievance.

The film's portrayal of the revolution emphasised the destruction of religious and civic institutions — the closure of churches, the suppression of the clergy, and the dismantling of the legal and commercial systems that had supported the middle class. The revolutionary committees were depicted as instruments of intimidation and arbitrary power, and the revolution's supporters were portrayed as mobs rather than as political actors with coherent objectives.

The exile experience was presented as a collective trauma. The Vives family's loss of home, property, status, and community was framed as representative of the broader Baliscan exile experience, and the film's final scenes — the family's reduced circumstances in Sant Cristòfor, the father's backward glance toward the sea — were intended to evoke the loss and longing of the entire Baliscano diaspora.

== Release and reception ==

The film premiered at the [[Sant Cristòfor Municipal Theatre]] in 1923 and was subsequently distributed to Volisanian-language cinemas across Sant Cristòfor. It was exported to [[Volisania]], [[Tapuya]], and [[Potocsí]] through the distribution networks established by the Baliscan exile community, and it became one of the most popular films in the Volisanian-speaking world.

The film's popularity was driven by the Baliscan diaspora, which constituted a significant audience across the Abyalan hemisphere. The film spoke directly to the exile community's experience of loss and displacement, and its anti-revolutionary message resonated with an audience that had personally suffered the consequences of the revolution. In Volisania, the film was received as a powerful indictment of revolutionary politics and was praised by conservative and moderate political commentators. In Tapuya and Potocsí, where Volisanian-speaking communities had grown through Baliscan immigration, the film was screened for years after its initial release.

The film was not without its critics. Some reviewers in Volisania noted that the film's portrayal of pre-revolution Balisca was idealised and that the revolution's supporters were depicted as caricatures rather than as complex characters. Others observed that the film's emotional power derived from its subject matter rather than from its technical achievements, which were modest even by the standards of the early 1920s. These criticisms did not diminish the film's commercial success.

The film could not be screened in [[Balisca]] itself, as the post-revolution government barred the distribution of films produced by Baliscan émigrés. Reports from Baliscan exiles who maintained contact with relatives in Balisca indicated that the film was known there through smuggled prints and private screenings, though the consequences of being caught with a copy of the film were severe.

== Legacy ==

''El Camí de l'Exili'' is considered the most significant film of the golden era of Balboan cinema and one of the most important cultural artefacts of the Baliscan exile community. The film's anti-revolutionary stance and its depiction of the exile experience made it a defining work for the Baliscano Balboan community, and it continued to be screened in Volisanian-language cinemas in Sant Cristòfor and across the Abyalan hemisphere for decades after its release.

The film is one of the few golden era productions that survives in complete form. A print was preserved by the [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]], which holds the only known complete copy. The film's survival is attributed to its cultural significance — the Baliscano Balboan community maintained multiple private prints, and at least one was donated to the archive after its establishment in 1956.

The film has been the subject of academic study in the fields of Balboan cinema history, Baliscan exile studies, and the political culture of the Volisanian-speaking world. Scholars have noted the film's role in shaping the exile community's collective memory of the revolution and in constructing a narrative of pre-revolution Baliscan society that emphasized its prosperity and social cohesion while minimising the social inequalities that contributed to the revolution's popular support.

In contemporary scholarship, the film is widely regarded as a work of propaganda. The deliberately idyllic portrayal of pre-revolution Baliscan life, the caricatured depiction of revolutionary supporters, and the complete absence of any acknowledgment of the social conditions that produced the revolution are understood as conscious artistic choices intended to serve a political function rather than to represent Baliscan society accurately. The film is now studied alongside other exile cultural productions as an example of how displaced communities construct retrospective narratives of their former societies — narratives that emphasise the lost world's virtues while erasing its conflicts and inequalities. Modern Baliscan film historians have been particularly critical of the film's omissions, noting that the pre-revolution Baliscan society depicted in the film never existed in the idealised form presented on screen, and that the film's enduring popularity in the Volisanian-speaking world helped to entrench a distorted understanding of the revolution's causes among audiences unfamiliar with Baliscan history.

